rozygous  nips with single allelic dropout the  genomic dna being the reference  has of course no allele drop out with  the  replica advanced we can see we have  somewhere between about 15  to 30 percent of the oil dropout with  the picoplex and the malbec  technologies for amplifying the dna you  can see we have much  higher allelic dropout so in conclusion  the refugee advanced dna single cell  kits  show a lower allele dropout rate than  other sorts of technologies  and we attribute this to a couple of  different factors the first factor is  the preservation of the cell  in the cryoprotect buffer which protects  the genomic dna  the second is the isothermal lysis that  we have  and the third is the multiple  displacement amplification with the 529  polymerase  all of these things added up together  lead to lower  allelic dropouts  we looked at several different  applications and what i'm going to show  you is some data for carrier mapping  that was done  in collaboration with milosev hornak at  repromeda biology park in the czech  republic  in this experiment biopsies which  contain a few cells were isolated from  the trophy epiderm of embryos  using a fine needle glass needle and  they were transferred into pcr  tubes so the workflow is to take the  biopsy  the biopsied cells which are a few cells  are washed three times in droplets of  the cryo-protective buffer  and 0.1 percent pvp the 0.1 percent pvp  is to keep the cells from adhering to  the pcr tubes  they are then after the wash preserve  put into one microliter of the cbp with  0.1 percent pvp  and then it is 20 degrees  following the storage the process by  adding 3 microliters of the pure  cbp they are then subjected to  the whole genome amplification using the  replica advanced dna single cell kit  and then their karyo map using the human  curio map 12 dna analysis kit  which is a microarray platform from  aluminum  through this study there were 10  different  cases that were analyzed and i'll show  y